pawlak@zeubac.desy.de (Jerzy Michal Pawlak) writes:


>In article <2uekhgINN3af@srvr1.engin.umich.edu>, mneylon@azure.engin.umich.edu (Michael K. Neylon) writes:

[Stuff about problems deleted... I agree with that!]
>Actually I think the last point culd be THE idea: When starting a game in
>'tournament' mode nethack generates all locations of special levels and
>a random number for every level and writes that to a file. Now when a player
>enters a tournament the file is read, an each time the player enters a new
>level, the random number generator is re-seeded with the number found in the
>for this level.
>[...]
>This is much easier and uses up less space then initial generation of the 
>whole dungeon .
True, but it still asks for major changes in the code. I really liked the idea
to start in wizard mode, wish lots of scrolls of teleportation and potions
of booze, visit all dungeon levels and then save the game. How much disk 
space is one dungeon? 1 M? I don't think that's too much for most of us.
And every player would have more or less the same problems: Bones levels
the same, initial objects the same, initial monster the same. It would
be more or less like visiting the same dungeon on a different day... That's
enough for competition, I think.
